================================================================================
DISPOSITION
================================================================================
A complete, HIG-native SwiftUI FeedbackQueuePage shipped at web parity: a pure @Observable
state holder (ADR-004 — no networking in the view) exposing the four list data states
(loading/empty/error/success) over an injectable FeedbackQueueDataSource seam whose value
types mirror the web FeedbackEntry / FeedbackListResponse / FeedbackUpdateInput 1:1 (the
method names echo the web hooks: loadFeedback<-useFeedbackList, updateFeedback<-useUpdateFeedback;
production binds the KMP feedback adapter through the seam). The page reproduces the web chrome
(PageContainer title) and the single GlassPanel1 holding the always-visible filter row
(status + category TSSelect dropdowns fed the snake_case query, a ghost Refresh control with a
fetch spinner, and the !bridgeEnabled hint) above the paginated, expandable feedback table.
The table is adaptive (columnar Grid on macOS/iPad, per-row cards on iPhone) across the seven
web columns — Created, Category badge, Title, Page route, Reporter (avatar + email), Status
badge, GitHub issue link — with the expandable FeedbackExpansion (report body, app-version/
user-agent/submitter/masked-email metadata grid, recent-errors + console-tail disclosures, and
the change-status / attach-url / save-url / forward-to-GitHub action row). Units are not
applicable (control-plane metadata); the timestamp + JSON are formatted at the display boundary
by FeedbackQueueFormat (ported from dateFormat.ts) and unit-tested. Every visible string
resolves from Localizable.xcstrings with the web key names (38 required keys). Verified on BOTH
idioms by isolated Swift-6 strict-concurrency type-checks (my files 0/0 macOS + iOS, exercising
the #if os(iOS) compact branches) and an executed model harness (all assertions passed; it also
caught + drove the fix of a real actor-isolation default-value bug). The full xcodebuild scheme
is RED ONLY due to the pre-existing, out-of-scope swift-frontend crash on the generated
TeslaSync/shared-surfaces mega-codebase (ZERO of my files; forbidden to commit-fix).

